WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Speaker 1: It's great to have.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:05.890
Back on the program today, Coleman Hughes,
who's a writer, opinion columnist and host

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:08.860
of the podcast Conversations with Coleman.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.850
Coleman, it's great to have you on again.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.530
Speaker 5: It's great to be on again.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:17.380
Speaker 1: So okay, let let's talk about something
that's very much in the news right now.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:24.770
A couple weeks ago, CNN reporter Jim Acosta
just kind of showed up and approached Republican

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:30.420
Congresswoman Marjorie Taylor GREENE on the
street and kind of imposed a series of questions

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.399
to her about her supposedly not remembering
things she said in the aftermath of the January

00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:40.050
6th riots or in the immediate period before
it.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:46.899
We now are in the midst of people, mostly
pro-choice protesters, showing up at the houses

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:51.769
of a few Supreme Court justices and protesting
at their homes.

00:00:51.769 --> 00:00:56.969
You also told me about a situation where right
wing activist Candace Owens showed up with

00:00:56.969 --> 00:01:02.219
a camera crew at the home of a Black Lives
Matter but person.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:07.090
I kind of want to talk through your thoughts
on these techniques, and I have a few thoughts

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:12.079
on them, although I don't have a sort of completely
defined opinion on them.

00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:16.939
I guess first, are these things, the three
instances I just mentioned.

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:19.850
Are they different or are they the same in
your mind?

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.060
And how would you approach thinking about
them?

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:28.350
Speaker 5: Yeah, that's it's interesting to
I haven't formulated my opinion on this either.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.950
So I guess there's one distinction to make
is approaching someone on the street as opposed

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.280
to knocking on their door.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.270
I'm not sure either is really worse than the
other, although.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:46.570
You know, one in a sense, knocking on my door
is more of an not quite an invasion of my

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:54.880
privacy, but a sort of questioning of coming
closer to to my private space.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:01.450
On the other hand, I'm actually more protected
behind my front door than I am on the street.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:07.470
And at a basic level, it's sort of no different
than a Jehovah's Witness coming and not knocking

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.630
on my front door and asking for some of my
attention.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:13.050
I can simply say, No, there's nothing illegal
about it.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.860
It's an annoyance.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.590
And it's it's unplanned.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.910
And there is something.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:26.940
Somewhat invasive about it, but I can't say
that there's actually anything truly unethical

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.940
about it.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.170
That would be my first pass at an opinion.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.670
It's the kind of thing that maybe I will think
less of the journalist who opts for this as

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:41.230
a Plan A. But on the other hand, maybe they've
reached out to this person ten times and the

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.130
person just won't engage with any media despite
being a public figure.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:49.580
So as a last resort, they're sort of trying
to catch them off guard.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:54.630
Speaker 1: That last thing is interesting
to me because my instinct has generally been,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.590
okay, this is not an approach I use.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:01.180
We make all sorts of interview requests and
sometimes they're ignored or sometimes they're

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.530
turned down.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.680
And I sort of see that as final, like the
person has turned down their opportunity to

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:07.680
talk to me.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:08.680
Right.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.860
But then at the same time, sometimes with
public servants or with people in positions

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.670
that you're appointed for life, you get the
feeling as just a citizen that they're in

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:24.030
a sense, beyond an accountability, that it
feels like maybe there should be.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:30.010
So, for example, Marjorie Taylor GREENE, salary
paid by the taxpayer.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.890
You know, we'll talk more about that because
that could apply to postal workers, too.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.720
Or, you know, somebody at the DMV, I don't
know.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.110
So we'll talk about whether that's relevant.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:46.090
And one of 435 legislators, someone who has
an impact on policy and law, etc., and who

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:51.580
can just say, I'm not going to answer questions
about what some believe was her dishonesty

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.200
at this hearing that took place in Georgia.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.880
And so is it wrong as a last resort for Jim
Acosta to show up there, the Supreme Court

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:06.630
justice appointed for life, essentially, I
mean, short of some kind of gross, demonstrable

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:12.220
misdeed, you know, there's no recourse for
saying we're going to remove a Supreme Court

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:13.220
justice.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:14.720
Yes, they can be impeached, but it doesn't
happen.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.410
So then you show up at the house.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:21.650
I don't know that I'm like 98% of the way
to this is not okay.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:26.630
And then the how do you have accountability
is where I kind of end up thinking twice.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:27.810
Speaker 5: Right.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.860
So you and I are in the same position as people
that interview a lot of people, public figures.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:38.930
I don't know how many politicians necessarily,
but we're in a position where we want to talk

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:43.960
to a lot of people that say no to us and our
audiences want us to talk to a lot of people

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.520
that say no to us.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.520
True.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:47.620
And I completely agree with you.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:52.730
Never in a million years would I show up at
someone's house or for instance, I wrote an

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:56.681
open letter to Ebrahim Kendi, the author,
to have a conversation with me.

00:04:56.681 --> 00:05:00.600
And I, in fact, you know, raised a go fund
me.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:09.540
I raised, you know, like 25 over $20,000 for
a charity to connect the event to that cause.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.980
And he still didn't want to do it.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:16.190
And it's of a piece with his not wanting to
talk to any of his critics, which I think

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:21.220
is a bad thing, but never in a million years
what I show up at this man's house or try

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.190
to cost him on the street.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:30.110
And I think it's because while it's it's I
guess it's no more complicated than the golden

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:31.110
rule.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.570
Like if someone wanted to talk to me and I
didn't want to talk to them and they showed

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:40.320
up at my apartment, I would view that as almost
hostile.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.320
Right.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:48.340
It's like as though you can say that it's
just the desire to talk, but it's the desire

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:52.050
to talk where they control the pace of everything.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.740
They knew they were there prepared.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.240
I might be in my pajamas for for all you know.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.170
And it's it's also.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:09.780
Close enough to a situation where there is
a mob outside my house where it's.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:10.780
It's.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:16.870
It's it's going to be very difficult for me
to be in a kind of relaxed state, to engage

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.980
with you on equal footing.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.650
It's a little bit like shooting downhill.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:27.620
It's like you have the high ground in in a
battle situation.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:31.550
And I don't want to be really in that position
with someone I'm talking to.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:37.910
So there is something really strange and almost
hostile about it.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:40.040
Speaker 1: Yeah, I would say not even almost.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.202
I mean, I don't know.

00:06:41.202 --> 00:06:46.340
I was at a restaurant a couple of months ago
and I told the story and someone was kind

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.760
of I could tell that they recognized me.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:54.449
And then as I was leaving, they approached
me and pretty loudly they said, You Jew imperialist

00:06:54.449 --> 00:07:00.210
bastard, which didn't even accurately reflect
my views on the nuances of the Israeli-Palestinian

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:01.390
conflict, as you can imagine.

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:03.640
Speaker 5: But violences of imperialism, yeah.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:09.240
Speaker 1: That that was not that was not
going no matter what.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.630
That's not going to be a real conversation.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:16.440
And I think maybe part of what you're getting
at is if this is the catalyst to start the

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.920
conversation, even if it's only supposed to
be a conversation, how good of one is it really

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:20.920
going to be?

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.220
Speaker 5: Yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.510
Which is, I think.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.610
Why?

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:28.270
I wouldn't do it and probably why you wouldn't
do it.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:35.360
But people that are more into sensationalist,
sensationalist journalism and people for whom

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:41.760
just getting the 62nd clip of whatever happens
is their bread and butter.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.370
Speaker 1: Now, what about the people who
just show up?

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:47.419
They're not journalists.

00:07:47.419 --> 00:07:51.090
The ones showing up at, like, you know, Kavanaugh's
house or whichever justice they're showing

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:55.360
up at, they're saying, well, the Supreme Court,
where all of these people are here for life,

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.490
have made a decision, assuming we've been
told the truth of the decision that's forthcoming.

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:01.650
They're making a decision I don't like.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.740
There's really no recourse with these folks.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.110
And you could say, well, you've got to make
sure at the state level then that abortion

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:13.270
stays legal because there won't be a federal
right and it starts to become a.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:18.680
Well, maybe it is okay to show up at their
house, although I would never do that.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:22.020
They would argue, what else can we really
do?

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.229
Speaker 5: Yeah.

00:08:24.229 --> 00:08:25.229
So.

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:33.479
In one sense, the that's actually a feature,
not a bug of American democracy, is that the

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:40.500
Supreme Court is supposed to be insulated
from that kind of intense public pressure.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:41.500
Yes.

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:48.759
You know, they are one of the institutions
that separate America from a direct democracy

00:08:48.759 --> 00:08:50.160
by design.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.829
They're not supposed to be 100% responsive
to public outrage or public reaction.

00:08:55.829 --> 00:08:59.770
They're supposed to be somewhat insulated
from that.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:04.460
So from that point of view, if you think that
future of American democracy is good, then

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:08.769
you probably shouldn't show up at outside
a Supreme Court justices house.

00:09:08.769 --> 00:09:14.389
And, you know, yeah, there are I mean, the
point of democracy, I think, is that we have

00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:15.589
these.

00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:21.379
I really view democracy not as a system that
produces the best policies all the time.

00:09:21.379 --> 00:09:23.379
I think that's actually too generous.

00:09:23.379 --> 00:09:29.420
I think what it does better than other systems
of government is channel the inevitable outrage

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:38.550
of different factions into a nonviolent process,
which is if you can vote, it releases a lot

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:45.220
of the steam and anger that humans will always
build up about intense issues.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:49.149
And it's like it's like a release valve on
that steam where you can go to the ballot

00:09:49.149 --> 00:09:50.720
box instead of the battlefield.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.629
And it's essentially a civil war prevention
mechanism, in my view.

00:09:56.629 --> 00:10:04.839
And so when I see things inching closer to
violence and which is not to say that people

00:10:04.839 --> 00:10:09.220
who show up outside these folks houses are
violent, but we know how these things sort

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:10.670
of spill over sometimes.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:15.889
Yeah, I do think it's it's worth encouraging
people to, you know, reminding them that we

00:10:15.889 --> 00:10:17.100
do live in a democracy.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:24.800
You do have, you know, electoral means of
influencing the laws in your state.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.860
And that's probably the wiser way to go.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:32.720
Speaker 1: This sort of connects also to the
issue of I mean, the the sort of prototypical

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:39.089
situation is a speaker goes to a college campus
and they were invited.

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:44.070
Often it's by a conservative group on campus,
which often is the minority on campus.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:48.100
I will readily admit, or at least on some
campuses, it's the minority.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:53.619
And then there are protesters against the
speaker, and sometimes they're outside the

00:10:53.619 --> 00:11:00.369
building, but sometimes they're inside and
will rise up as the speech is about to start.

00:11:00.369 --> 00:11:07.879
And to me, that's not really about free speech
per say in the sense of nobody has a right

00:11:07.879 --> 00:11:09.480
to speak at a college.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:13.069
So not being invited is not a violation of
anything.

00:11:13.069 --> 00:11:18.949
But at the same time, if you're invited, it
seems to me that it's reasonable to expect

00:11:18.949 --> 00:11:23.550
that you can at least get your thoughts out
and then during question and answer or whatever.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.300
But then the counter always is when I kind
of go this direction.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:31.619
COLEMAN People saying it's not if you just
stick to the norms, you'll never really get

00:11:31.619 --> 00:11:32.619
anything done.

00:11:32.619 --> 00:11:40.100
By definition, activism that has an impact
may be inclusive of speech that isn't comfortable

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.220
or isn't being done exactly when it's allowed.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:46.380
And it gets to the idea of the free speech
zone that was generated during George W Bush's

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.300
presidency, where, yes, you're allowed to
protest.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:54.379
But in this box a hundred feet from the venue,
for example, how do you think that ties in

00:11:54.379 --> 00:11:59.759
in terms of the speech of the speaker versus
the speech of those who say, I don't like

00:11:59.759 --> 00:12:00.759
this speaker?

00:12:00.759 --> 00:12:01.759
Speaker 5: Yeah.

00:12:01.759 --> 00:12:06.639
So the first thing I would say is I think
there's a big difference between protesting

00:12:06.639 --> 00:12:10.600
inside the event which drowns out the speech
and protesting outside.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:15.699
At Columbia, I witnessed lots of protests
like robust protests outside.

00:12:15.699 --> 00:12:19.290
And those are totally, totally valid.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:21.350
And they get the point across.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:26.350
They definitely get the point across that
a lot of people object to the speaker.

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:31.630
But I've said you don't have the right to
just interrupt any event that you want because

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:34.589
you hate the person and drown it out.

00:12:34.589 --> 00:12:35.589
So.

00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:38.480
But to to your second point.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:42.399
This idea that, you know, you have to play
dirty to get things done.

00:12:42.399 --> 00:12:43.399
Right.

00:12:43.399 --> 00:12:49.279
And the other side is there's bigotry and
there's evil and there's all this stuff.

00:12:49.279 --> 00:12:53.439
So if we play by the rules, if we, you know,
we're just going to get whatever.

00:12:53.439 --> 00:12:55.079
I don't I really disagree with that.

00:12:55.079 --> 00:13:00.949
I mean, if you look at, for one thing, the
most successful protest movement, I would

00:13:00.949 --> 00:13:05.739
argue, in the history of the country, which
was the civil rights movement.

00:13:05.739 --> 00:13:12.399
They they they took it very seriously to play
by certain norms.

00:13:12.399 --> 00:13:16.850
Because they understood that it was actually
strategically important to do so.

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:23.160
So, for example, during the March on Washington,
which was organized by Bayard Rustin and the

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:28.059
famous 63 March on Washington, where Martin
Luther King gave the I Have a Dream speech.

00:13:28.059 --> 00:13:34.449
Bayard Rustin At the end of that day, she
went to a Philip Randolph and.

00:13:34.449 --> 00:13:41.610
Like embraced him and congratulated him on
the fact that there was not a single crime

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:44.670
at the whole event with thousands and thousands
of people.

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:48.119
You understand what a difficult thing it is
to manage that.

00:13:48.119 --> 00:13:56.379
Like BLM had so many protests and I went to
many in New York where at the beginning a

00:13:56.379 --> 00:13:59.619
local BLM leader would say this is a peaceful
protest.

00:13:59.619 --> 00:14:02.170
They would emphasize that with a bullhorn.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:07.720
And still there would always be five or ten
people that ended up doing something crazy

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:09.360
and starting right.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:14.929
So it's incredibly difficult to actually police
the boundaries within a protest and make sure

00:14:14.929 --> 00:14:20.449
no one crosses those boundaries so that you
come across principled and persuasive to people

00:14:20.449 --> 00:14:21.790
that don't already agree with you.

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:26.439
I think many protesters, they underestimate
the backlash phenomenon, which is when you

00:14:26.439 --> 00:14:31.980
go too far, when you demonstrate that you're
willing to like say, you know, like Charles

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:37.670
Murray and Allison Stanger when that happened,
that overland right to initiate violence,

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:46.120
you lose a lot of of good faith among persuadable
people that see you as as violent and dangerous

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:48.630
revolutionaries that should be in charge of
nothing.

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:49.630
Right.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:54.339
And even if a lot of people will only silently
form that opinion, I think a lot of modern

00:14:54.339 --> 00:14:59.860
protest movements fail to recognize that wisdom,
which civil rights leaders really understood

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:00.860
deeply.

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:04.910
Speaker 1: I guess I, I'm generally sensitive
to that perspective.

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:12.559
The the counter that I have is that there
are a non-significant there is a not insignificant

00:15:12.559 --> 00:15:17.490
contingent which will frame any protest as
violent anyway.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.499
Now, that doesn't justify any violence.

00:15:19.499 --> 00:15:22.279
I'm still against any violence and criminality.

00:15:22.279 --> 00:15:27.559
But also, even if you say, okay, well, we're
not going to do any physical violence, but

00:15:27.559 --> 00:15:33.000
we will, for example, shut down a road to
make our presence more known.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.189
Because if we just stay on the sidewalk, we're
really not even going to have the impact that

00:15:37.189 --> 00:15:38.279
we think we need.

00:15:38.279 --> 00:15:42.480
I think the concern is that there are actors
who will say whatever is being done other

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:47.129
than basically being invisible is wrong and
is a form of violence regardless.

00:15:47.129 --> 00:15:50.410
And then the question becomes, how do you
deal with the bad faith criticisms that will

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:51.499
come either way?

00:15:51.499 --> 00:15:53.309
Speaker 5: Yeah.

00:15:53.309 --> 00:15:56.189
I mean, to me, I think there is.

00:15:56.189 --> 00:16:01.279
You know, if I were in charge of a protest
movement.

00:16:01.279 --> 00:16:02.279
Sure.

00:16:02.279 --> 00:16:11.970
I think that this strategic wise route is
to demonstrate moral seriousness.

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:19.190
That is, you know, every every protest movement
that has really looked back on as something

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:25.269
that made major gains for society, whether
it be Gandhi in India or the civil rights

00:16:25.269 --> 00:16:31.860
movement or the marriage equality movement.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:32.860
It it.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:38.619
Key to their success was that they demonstrated
that they were morally serious people that

00:16:38.619 --> 00:16:43.629
wanted to improve and not simply tear down.

00:16:43.629 --> 00:16:48.749
And the way to do that, even if you're even
if you're disobeying the law in the context

00:16:48.749 --> 00:16:52.600
of civil disobedience, is like Rosa Parks
disobeyed the law.

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:54.290
Martin Luther King disobeyed the law.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:57.189
They got arrested dozens and dozens of times.

00:16:57.189 --> 00:16:58.189
They were.

00:16:58.189 --> 00:17:00.310
In that sense, they were not.

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:08.149
I mean, they were not just acquiescing to
anything or they were willing to disobey laws

00:17:08.149 --> 00:17:09.519
all the time.

00:17:09.519 --> 00:17:14.439
But precisely because they were doing that,
they took very seriously the aim to demonstrate

00:17:14.439 --> 00:17:16.650
that they were morally serious people.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.420
They were not disobeying the law just to disobey
the law.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:23.100
They were disobeying it because they thought
it needed to be changed.

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:24.429
And they were right.

00:17:24.429 --> 00:17:30.980
And it's again, there are so many failed protests,
movements, protest movements that that get

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.649
very little accomplished.

00:17:32.649 --> 00:17:34.559
I think.

00:17:34.559 --> 00:17:39.540
It would be very unwise to not observe the
differences between the ones that work and

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:40.540
the ones that don't.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:41.540
And that's one of the big ones.

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:49.350
Speaker 1: Are there specific so part of the
examples you gave as positively include a

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:55.800
sort of assessment by you partially with the
benefit of hindsight now as to kind of the

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:56.870
intentions?

00:17:56.870 --> 00:18:01.889
And I'm wondering in terms of tactics, I mean,
I think sometimes in environmental protests

00:18:01.889 --> 00:18:07.960
there's the chaining oneself to ax and sometimes
it's a tree or sometimes it's the fence at

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:10.860
a nuclear power plant or whatever the case
may be.

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:16.419
Does the tactic itself tell you much about
the the moral concerned?

00:18:16.419 --> 00:18:21.350
Speaker 5: Yeah, I think the tactic tells
you a lot about the moral concerns.

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:28.139
So, for instance, if the tactic is maybe too
obvious to say, but if the tactic is violent,

00:18:28.139 --> 00:18:33.140
it automatically legitimates the moral concern
in most people's eyes.

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:34.140
Okay.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:39.250
It's like if your even, you know, I guess
this is unless.

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:41.120
Right.

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:46.289
So if you're just a terrorist, if you're an
environmental terrorist and you shoot somebody,

00:18:46.289 --> 00:18:47.289
no one is going.

00:18:47.289 --> 00:18:49.610
Very few people are going to be persuaded.

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:54.049
Certainly Congress is not going to be persuaded
by that kind of a protest.

00:18:54.049 --> 00:19:00.470
If your tactic is nonviolent, if you spell
out exactly what your principles are and why

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:04.000
you're violating some law.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:08.200
Then that demonstrates a kind of moral seriousness.

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:11.100
And that's an actual appeal to the public.

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:12.100
Right.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:20.740
And moreover, to be willing to suffer on behalf
of your principles, like to Martin Luther

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:26.570
King was so committed to nonviolence in a
way that requires such extreme restraint,

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:27.570
right.

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:32.419
To literally be beaten and saying that's the
ultimate demonstration of moral seriousness.

00:19:32.419 --> 00:19:37.770
It's like, I'm so serious about this moral
principle and getting it in the right means

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:41.580
that I'm willing to subject myself to this.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.740
That was extremely persuasive to people.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:44.840
Mm hmm.

